Body:
Another workaround is to use a very simple tool, like this:
```console
$ cat .opencode/tool/get-current-session-id.ts 
import { tool } from "@opencode-ai/plugin"

export default tool({
  description: "Get the current session ID",
  args: {},
  async execute(args, context) {
    return context.sessionID
  },
})
```

I’ve been running into the same issue when using OpenCode with OpenRouter + OpenAI models (e.g. `openrouter/openai/gpt-5.1-codex`).

After digging into the logs and request payload, I confirmed that the root cause is:

- OpenCode re-sends prior "assistant" messages including the `reasoning` / `reasoning_details` metadata
- When OpenRouter forwards that to OpenAI, the encrypted reasoning blocks (`providerMetadata` with `reasoning.encrypted` items like `rs_...`) no longer match the OpenAI project/key OpenRouter is using
- OpenAI then rejects the request with:

  > The encrypted content for item rs_0aa9cdb85cd23c86016920cad04e4c8196bf0bdb7dbce6a4df could not be verified.
  > type: `invalid_request_error`

To work around this, I patched my local OpenCode clone to *strip reasoning provider metadata only when using the OpenRouter provider*.

Concretely, I added an option to `MessageV2.toModelMessage(...)`:

```ts
export function toModelMessage(
  input: { info: Info; parts: Part[] }[],
  options?: {
    // When true, strips reasoning providerMetadata to avoid OpenAI encrypted content verification errors via OpenRouter
    stripReasoningMetadata?: boolean
  },
): ModelMessage[] { ... }
```

and when `model.npm === "@openrouter/ai-sdk-provider"`, I call it with `stripReasoningMetadata: true`.

Inside `toModelMessage`, for **assistant** messages I now do (simplified):

```ts
if (part.type === "text")
  assistantMessage.parts.push({
    type: "text",
    text: part.text,
    providerMetadata: options?.stripReasoningMetadata ? undefined : part.metadata,
  })

if (part.type === "tool") {
  ...
  callProviderMetadata: options?.stripReasoningMetadata ? undefined : part.metadata,
}

if (part.type === "reasoning") {
  assistantMessage.parts.push({
    type: "reasoning",
    text: part.text,
    // Skip providerMetadata when stripReasoningMetadata is true to avoid
    // OpenAI encrypted content verification errors when using OpenRouter
    providerMetadata: options?.stripReasoningMetadata ? undefined : part.metadata,
  })
}
```

So the encrypted reasoning blocks are no longer forwarded when going through OpenRouter, but everything else (including the reasoning text itself) is preserved. Other providers (direct OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.) keep the original behavior.

With this patch in place, I can now use `openrouter/openai/gpt-5.1-codex` in OpenCode without hitting the “Provider returned error / encrypted content could not be verified” issue.

If it’s helpful, I’m happy to turn this into a PR — or feel free to adapt this approach in whatever way best fits the existing provider abstractions.

